Tension Myositis Syndrome
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Chronic recurrent severe back/neck pain, Strong correlation with severe psychological stress, Migrating pain lacking specific dermatomal distribution, Normal structural spinal MRI

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-047
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, and incapacitating lower back pain causing sudden functional immobility and intense panic regarding a spinal injury.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with an acute, severe exacerbation of unremitting back pain resulting in an absolute inability to stand or walk. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively perform a neurological examination, exclude acute cauda equina syndrome, spinal cord compression, or an acute disc herniation, and immediately manage the acute functional collapse before daytime hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My lower back just seized up completely solid, I can't move my legs without agonizing pain, I think I've slipped a disc and I'm paralyzed."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a 4-hour severe acute exacerbation of chronic, recurrent, and severe unremitting pain predominantly localized to the lower back. The patient describes an intense localized muscle stiffness and a profound feeling of severe regional tightness that occurred suddenly following a highly stressful phone call regarding a family dispute (repressed emotional stress/rage). The pain is currently causing absolute avoidance of physical activity, driven primarily by an intense fear of causing severe physical damage to the spine. The patient notes a shifting, migrating nature of the severe pain over the past year. The patient complains of vague tingling in the legs, but explicitly denies loss of bowel or bladder control.
Functional Status: Lying rigidly flat on the floor, absolutely refusing to move or attempt to stand.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound psychological distress; high risk of ongoing immobility and secondary deconditioning.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Visibly terrified, breathing shallowly to avoid moving the torso, lying supine on the living room floor.
Physical Exam: Comprehensive neurological examination of the lower limbs is completely normal. Motor power is 5/5 globally (hip flexion, knee extension, dorsiflexion, plantarflexion). Deep tendon reflexes (patellar, Achilles) are 2+ and perfectly symmetrical. Sensation to light touch and pinprick is intact globally, lacking any specific objective neurological dermatomal deficit. Straight leg raise test is negative for radicular pain (elicits only generalized lumbar muscle tightness). Palpation of the lumbar paraspinals reveals severe, diffuse, bilateral muscle hypertonicity without point tenderness over the spinous processes. Documented history of a completely normal structural spinal MRI, normal EMG, and normal inflammatory blood panels.
Environmental Observations: House is tense; patient's phone is thrown across the room following the triggering argument.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications; terrified to take painkillers.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Tension Myositis Syndrome - Status: Acute psychosomatic muscle spasm
Differentials:

Cauda Equina Syndrome - Ruled out explicitly by the normal lower limb motor/sensory exam, intact symmetrical reflexes, and complete absence of saddle anesthesia or bowel/bladder incontinence.

Acute Lumbar Radiculopathy (Herniated Disc) - Ruled out clinically by the negative straight leg raise test, completely normal reflexes, and non-dermatomal, migrating distribution of the vague tingling.

Ankylosing Spondylitis - Ruled out by the sudden, stress-triggered onset (rather than insidious morning stiffness improving with exercise) and historically normal inflammatory markers/MRI.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours assessment due to a severe, acute functional collapse and extreme panic resulting from intense psychosomatic back pain mimicking a catastrophic spinal cord injury. The absolute inability to walk mandated an immediate, hands-on neurological examination. By executing a thorough neurological assessment and definitively confirming entirely normal, intact spinal cord and nerve root function, the physician safely excluded a life-threatening spinal emergency. Delivering this concrete, physical proof of structural safety immediately de-escalated the severe psychological crisis, preventing a highly distressed and completely unnecessary emergency department transfer for advanced neuroimaging. This intervention strictly fulfills the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided direct, firm clinical reassurance of an entirely structurally normal, healthy spine based on physical examination. Assisted the patient in safely standing and walking across the room using graded exposure. Prescribed a short course of oral Diazepam 2mg BID for 3 days to break the intense skeletal muscle guarding.
Positive Behavioral Change: When the intense back pain spikes, instead of immediately lying down in fear, actively stand up and perform 2 minutes of brisk, assertive walking. Confronting the pain with physical movement directly proves to the hyperactive nervous system that the spine is structurally safe and not damaged, rewarding the patient with a rapid decrease in the fear-driven muscle spasm and restoring physical confidence.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a clinical psychologist specializing in chronic pain and somatic symptom disorders.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week to continue psychological and physical rehabilitation strategies.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing sudden, true weakness in the legs (an actual inability to lift the feet or stand), absolute numbness in the groin/saddle area, or any sudden loss of bowel or bladder control.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on the absolutely normal neurological examination today; any development of true focal weakness requires immediate spinal re-evaluation.
Clinical Course: Tension Myositis Syndrome is a severe physical muscle spasm directly generated by the brain in response to overwhelming emotional stress. The pain is 100% real and excruciating, but the spine itself is perfectly healthy and structurally unbreakable.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if your legs genuinely give out and you cannot bear weight, if you go numb around your groin, or if you lose control of your bladder or bowels.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The neurological tests we just performed prove that your spinal cord and nerves are in perfect working order. The severe pain you feel is caused by your brain deliberately clamping your lower back muscles completely tight due to the extreme stress of your recent argument. Because your spine is safe, resting in bed is the worst thing you can do; it reinforces the brain's false belief that you are injured.
Actionable Steps: Tomorrow morning, you must perform your normal daily routine, including walking and bending, regardless of the pain. Deliberately moving normally acts as physical proof to your nervous system that you are safe, forcing the brain to eventually release the severe muscle cramp.

